00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.500
in a consistent fashion.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:46.220
So from an internet-scale
application architecture,

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.380
at the end of the
day, we all want

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:54.140
to be able to push releases
n number of times each night.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:56.660
We want to be able to
build applications that

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:59.060
are coherently resilient
and scale really

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.400
well without having to
worry about the underlying

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.700
infrastructure.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:06.590
But as a developer, all you
really want to care about

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:08.740
is your application, right?

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.210
You really don't want to
care about the underlying

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:14.660
mechanisms, like your
networking, your load balance,

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:18.290
your health checks, how you
implement that resilient

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.400
architecture.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.770
Of course, it's
important, but you really

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:25.940
want to focus on the application
and then let the underlying

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:28.820
framework really do a
lot of the heavy lifting

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.100
to abstract away
the complexities

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:34.190
for that application
underneath that application.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:36.080
And really, that's
where Kubernetes

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.840
came into the picture.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.410
Containers solve a
very important problem

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:45.230
in terms of how do you
package up an application,

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:48.410
make it portable, run it
in such a way where you're

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.000
taking all these
various dependencies

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.710
and you have a really
predictable way

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:54.830
of running that application
across different environments.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:57.110
But then it introduces
a lot of complexities,

00:06:57.110 --> 00:07:00.140
such as, how do you
scale these applications,

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:04.490
how do you run these containers
across a pool of computers?

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.006
Containers are ephemeral--
how do you mount storage

00:07:07.006 --> 00:07:08.630
to these containers
and ensure that you

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:11.090
can run not just
12-factor stateless

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:13.940
applications, but stateful
applications as well.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:16.220
How do you do that in
a consistent fashion?

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:21.050
What about day two type models
around operations, monitoring,

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:22.490
logging.

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:25.130
How do you expose containers
to the outside world

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:29.030
so that they can be consumed
by external applications?

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.700
Those are the kinds of
things that Kubernetes really

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:33.260
helps solve.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.800
And Google has had a
managed Kubernetes offering

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:38.450
for the last three years.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:41.450
It went GA-- General
Availability-- three years ago,

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:43.430
called Google
Kubernetes Engine, which

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:46.460
is a managed service
around Kubernetes that

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:51.170
basically makes the cluster
creation process an API call.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.330
You specify how
many nodes you want,

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.490
where you want
your nodes to live,

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:59.360
the type of resiliency and
highly available infrastructure

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:01.340
that you want
underneath your cluster,

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:05.180
and then you're in business--
you've got an API, a command

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:08.030
line interface, that you can
now use to deploy and run

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:09.630
your applications.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:14.780
So what I wanted to do now
