00:09:23.925 --> 00:09:25.300
ALEX VAN BOXEL:
Thank you, Allan.

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:28.260
So I'm Alex.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:29.390
I'm a cloud architect.

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:33.060
So in a company at
our country and size,

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.390
that means you also have
to get your hands dirty,

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:38.340
and you also code.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:43.170
So but let me first
present Vente-Exclusive.

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:48.610
It's a French name, so
it's Vente-Exclusive.

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:52.680
Vente-Exclusive is a special
kind of e-commerce business,

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:53.880
and is flash sales.

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.010
What does that mean?

00:09:56.010 --> 00:10:01.320
We have, every day, like
100 sales open and close.

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:03.770
Each day we have opening
and closing sales.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:08.110
On average, a sale is
open for around a week.

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:11.400
So as soon as that
sale closes, the goods

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:15.760
are ordered with the suppliers
and shipped to our customers.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:20.340
So it's not the next
day delivery in general.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:24.660
So about two years ago. we were
acquired by a bigger company.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:29.610
And that acquisition actually
brought some special things.

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:33.240
We could operate
in northern Europe.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:36.430
It was a French company
that acquired us,

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:39.990
so that means
everything above France.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:47.910
And that acquisition brought
some special cases for us,

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.070
because we were
normally operating

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:52.470
in a small part of Europe.

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:54.090
And we're running a monolith.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:56.160
And going to the
north of Europe,

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:00.090
that means more countries
with special business needs,

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.000
more languages, and
other currencies.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:07.200
Not all of Europe
is running the euro.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.470
So with that, we
said we're going

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.750
to develop the
platform of the future

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.810
and go for microservices.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:20.430
Running a monolith for 10 years
brought some extra advantages.

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.740
We actually knew our
business quite well.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:27.720
So we put silos in
our architecture

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:31.290
that was the shop,
the warehouse, data.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:34.200
And within those
silos, everybody

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.740
could make their
microservices on a certain use

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:42.110
case or domain.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:46.180
The technology that
could be used there

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:48.640
could be really
tailored to the use case

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:50.120
or the competence of the team.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:53.890
As it required mainly
a .NET company,

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:58.560
most of our services were
created in .NET But like

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:02.270
in the data team
with data scientists,

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:04.540
Python is more in
their skill sets.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:08.320
So there it turned out that
we had a lot of Python code

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:12.985
and even Go if we really
needed to have performance.

00:12:12.985 --> 00:12:19.060
The databases, it was also the
choice of the best use case.

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:22.732
So it could be relational
or non-relational.

00:12:22.732 --> 00:12:23.440
It didn't matter.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:25.930
They had their own choice,
but a very important thing

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:31.330
is that each microservice
is data separated.

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:33.601
So a very good
isolation between them.

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:40.430
And at the time, we started
more than a year ago

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.560
to re-platforming.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:45.710
And why did we pick Kubernetes?

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:49.300
Well, because at
the time that we

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:55.550
chose, until Kubernetes came
along, and Docker came along,

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:58.160
containerization was
really a technical thing.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:00.770
And Kubernetes were
the first orchestrators

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:02.300
that actually
brought some concepts

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:04.740
that a human could understand.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:10.970
And luckily we were already on
Google with our data platforms,

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:14.150
so we actually tried out GKE.

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:20.030
We also asked for an offer on
our current hosting provider,

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:23.630
but by the time that they
came with a real offer

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:28.920
we were already running
some stuff on GKE.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:32.650
But going to microservices
brings some extra challenges.

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:34.830
So it's more than
three, but I picked

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:36.900
three of my favorites ones.

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:40.400
It's like the developer
experience, observability,

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.060
and also the data silos.

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:51.280
Developer experience--
so if you're

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:53.440
used to creating a
monolith and then

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:56.560
have to go to make
very big blocks,

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:01.310
you have to make sure the
developers don't lose speed.

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:05.260
So in the beginning, we
did a lot of small things,

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:10.390
and made sure that
the development

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:12.640
speed didn't go too low.

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:16.330
So the first thing
was access control.

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:19.210
Luckily, Google had
the Google accounts

00:14:19.210 --> 00:14:22.960
that we really could sync
with our on-premise active

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:24.280
directory.

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:29.530
And it was with just a snap
they have access to the cluster.

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:31.570
And with those Google
accounts, they also

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:36.490
have access to services
like logging, tracing,

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.000
and even if they
needed BigQuery,

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:39.580
they could have
access to BigQuery.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:45.180
Another thing that
is very important

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:48.450
and helped us in
the beginning, was

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:54.500
to make sure that you have a
set of guidelines between teams.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:56.910
It's very important.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:00.860
One of them is, make sure
that your microservice has

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:02.190
a single name.

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:05.490
You could have some silly
names like boreporting,

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:07.180
or is it back office deporting.

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:10.830
But the thing is
that it really helps

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:13.100
with automating the process.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:17.300
So with conventions, when
there was the API at the back,

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:21.135
then we automatically exposed
that to the outside world.

00:15:21.135 --> 00:15:21.760
Was it a batch?

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:24.420
No, it was a batch system.

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:31.080
It really helps to just make
the developer flow go well.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:36.750
Another thing is, make sure
that you have contracts defined

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:38.780
between the microservices.

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:41.940
We actually learned the hard
way that going [? good ?] first

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:43.780
is not the best approach.

00:15:43.780 --> 00:15:48.300
So we are actually slowly
moving now to a contract first,

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:52.830
where we are slowly
moving to gRPC.

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:56.040
And with some cool tools
like Cloud Endpoints

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:00.150
and probably [INAUDIBLE]
as well, it eases the way.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:04.410
Because it can expose your gRPC
interfaces as a REST as well.

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:11.080
Then, don't forget this--

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:12.520
continuous deployment.

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:15.820
Start in the beginning.

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:17.650
All those conventions,
and so on,

00:16:17.650 --> 00:16:20.680
really help automate
the process.

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:22.480
Automation processes--
but make sure

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:24.970
that those automation
processes are all

00:16:24.970 --> 00:16:29.290
centered around the continuous
deployments pipeline.

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:35.710
We chose GitLab because it is a
nice combination with git repos

00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:38.410
and deployment pipelines.

00:16:38.410 --> 00:16:42.690
And it has some nice tools
for code reviews as well.

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:44.780
We are now going
to the next level.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:47.010
And with the evolution
of Kubernetes,

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:49.220
with the custom
resource definitions,

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:50.890
we're going for
the Git approach,

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:53.510
GitOps approach,
where everything,

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:55.340
the desired state is
in the git itself.

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.355
Next, observability.

00:17:01.355 --> 00:17:05.359
As everything is
scattered around,

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:09.099
make sure that your
system is observable.

00:17:09.099 --> 00:17:12.740
In a single application
it's a bit simpler.

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:17.109
But luckily, Google has
a stack driver suite.

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:19.079
You probably saw it yesterday.

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:23.270
There were some nice
announcements here.

00:17:23.270 --> 00:17:26.119
So that's very good.

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:29.660
So ever every microservice--

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:32.030
for us, we had the
Cloud Endpoints.

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:35.160
But now with Istio, probably
you would go to Istio.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:37.830
But we have been running this
for a year on Cloud Endpoints.

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:41.450
It really helped us
to make it observable.

00:17:41.450 --> 00:17:45.570
Because having a proxy just
in front of your microservice

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:48.710
makes sure that everything
is locked and traced.

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:52.000
And that really helps.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:54.990
Those locks and so are
aggregated with everything

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:56.240
in the Google ecosystem.

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:58.720
So everything that comes
in the network and even

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:01.710
your Kubernetes cluster,
all come together

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:03.540
in the same logging system.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:07.090
And it really helps dive deep.

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:12.130
But don't forget, make sure that
you have like a special lock

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:19.720
entry points, trace
points, and metrics as well

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:20.710
in your application.

00:18:23.767 --> 00:18:26.100
An example of a metric is
like [? active ?] [? cards, ?]

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:27.550
for example.

00:18:27.550 --> 00:18:30.220
And through the
stack drive suite,

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:34.550
it all merges the operational
locks and your custom metrics

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:37.860
together to have a deep dive.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:41.670
And if you want to be
an observer superstar.

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:46.870
Make sure to export all
your locks to BigQuery.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:49.330
I find it quite strange that
not a lot of people do that,

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:50.980
but it's just a click away.

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:58.664
And now is the time to go
to Istio, look at that one.

00:18:58.664 --> 00:19:00.080
We were looking
at Cloud Endpoints

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:02.720
because we started very early.

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:04.550
Make sure to look at the Istio.

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:10.055
And also make sure to
look at OpenCensus.

00:19:10.055 --> 00:19:13.100
With that you get
nice dashboards.

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:14.850
Now, it's an answer
from yesterday,

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:17.420
you also have service
graphs added on top of it.

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:22.160
And that's very
important if you want

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:27.850
to dig deep if you have
troubles in your system.

00:19:27.850 --> 00:19:31.080
And the last, but this
is my favorite one,

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:36.140
is how to get the flow of
your data in your system.

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:39.720
So everybody knows the typical
use case of sync calls,

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:43.130
where microservice A
calls B, and B and C.

00:19:43.130 --> 00:19:46.580
But make sure to go for
an async approach as well.

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:49.100
Where you actually put
everything on Pub/Sub.

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:51.630
Pub/Sub is also a good
technology of Goggle,

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:55.280
where it just can
publish your entities.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:57.920
And that makes it possible
for other microservices

00:19:57.920 --> 00:19:59.330
to subscribe there.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:01.730
An example is when
a card is complete,

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:05.210
your logistic process could
listen on those events,

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:06.380
and trigger the slow flows.

00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:12.000
And with the added
value, if you do it

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.460
consistently over all
your microservices,

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:21.540
you could listen there in your
data lake on all those topics

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:26.470
and combine everything into
BigQuery, BigTable, or Elastic,

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:30.120
or whatever
technology you choose.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:32.790
And that's a way,
where you actually

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:37.140
break the barriers
between those silos.

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:40.440
And then your data analysts,
and your marketeers

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:43.890
really have one tool to
query over everything.

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:45.390
And that's then BigQuery.

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:50.790
And it combines, and you have
some nice dashboarding tools,

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:54.055
like Tableau, where you can have
backend view on the business.

00:20:56.710 --> 00:21:00.610
So in hindsight, going
to Kubernetes engine

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:02.800
was really a lifesaver,
because then we

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:05.410
could focus on those problems.

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:08.165
Because we like having
a managed service

00:21:08.165 --> 00:21:11.920
is not just throwing
Kubernetes on a few VMs.

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:15.850
No, it brings autoscaler,
autorepair, monitoring, all out

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:16.940
of the box.

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:20.140
And with the three monthly
release cycle of Kubernetes,

00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:23.340
the newest version of
Kubernetes is just a click away.

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:27.100
I do an upgrade of the cluster
to a new version in a meeting.

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:35.950
So lessons learned-- make sure
to have conventions, contracts,

00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:38.830
and automation in place.

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:42.040
That will help you
accelerate your developers.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:44.770
If you don't have
those things, you

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:48.520
could be up for
some [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:52.990
And embrace eventual
consistency through Pub/Sub,

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:55.400
where you publish
every entity in there,

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:59.830
and listen there in your data
lake and put it in BigQuery.

00:21:59.830 --> 00:22:02.440
And make your system observable.

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:05.360
Certainly with the nice
news announced yesterday

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:09.040
this will be great.

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:11.240
So that's it for me.

00:22:11.240 --> 00:22:12.690
Wei, it's up to you.

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:18.580
WEI LIEN DANG: Thank you.

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:20.410
My name is Wei Lien Dang.

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:22.730
I am VP of Product at StackRox.

00:22:22.730 --> 00:22:26.150
StackRox is a Bay
Area based startup

00:22:26.150 --> 00:22:29.600
that delivers cloud native
security for the Global 2000.

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:32.420
Our product is a platform
that secures containers

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:35.090
and microservices across
their entire lifecycle

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:37.760
from build to runtime.

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:40.340
We're pleased to be a
Google Cloud Partner

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:42.560
and jointly working with
some of the world's most

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:46.680
recognizable enterprises across
technology, media, government,

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:48.680
and financial services.

00:22:48.680 --> 00:22:51.140
This morning I'll share
about our collaboration

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:54.770
with financial organizations,
a collaboration that

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:58.340
has enabled them to modernize
their infrastructure and app

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:01.070
architectures atop
Kubernetes and GKE.

00:23:01.070 --> 00:23:04.190
And one of the primary
challenges that these customers

00:23:04.190 --> 00:23:07.520
face when moving to GKE
is thinking through how

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:11.090
to effectively leverage a shared
security model, which requires

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:14.550
considering what controls
do you need to implement

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:16.880
on a standalone
basis versus where

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:20.240
you need to augment existing
controls that are provided

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:21.800
by GKE.

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:25.740
And our customers tend to hone
in on the following concerns.

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:29.210
The first is, can strong
security boundaries

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:32.210
be established to
isolate and segregate

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:33.845
containerized workloads?

00:23:33.845 --> 00:23:37.640
And isolation needs to extend
across pods, and services,

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:40.770
nodes and clusters, and so on.

00:23:40.770 --> 00:23:44.750
Second, what is needed to ensure
that the container software

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:48.050
supply chain, which
generates the images used

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:50.270
to instantiate
containers, remains

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:52.810
under the customer's control?

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:56.180
You need continuous
verification, attestation,

00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:58.040
and auditability.

00:23:58.040 --> 00:23:59.990
And finally, this
customer segment

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:03.110
has to tackle regulatory
compliance in a context

00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:05.540
where requirements
for containers

00:24:05.540 --> 00:24:08.140
specifically are not
yet that well defined.

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:11.630
And so, relevant certifications
and data protection

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:15.120
requirements are going to vary
across workloads of course.

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:17.750
But what we tend to see is
that financial customers take

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:21.020
conservative interpretations
of existing requirements,

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:23.870
map them to container
environments, and also

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:26.660
increasingly adhere to
guidance on container

00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:30.950
security that has been published
by organizations such as NIST

00:24:30.950 --> 00:24:34.910
and the Center for
Internet Security.

00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:38.870
I'm excited this morning to
share three examples of how

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:41.480
the combination of
GKE and StackRox

00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:44.960
together has successfully
enabled enterprises

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:47.510
to navigate these concerns.

00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:51.620
In the first example, we've
worked with a Fortune 100 bank.

00:24:51.620 --> 00:24:53.360
That bank has a line
of business that's

00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:55.100
leading the bank's
digital transformation

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:59.120
efforts to re-imagine
the consumer experience.

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:01.670
And to do that, they've built
an entirely new investment

00:25:01.670 --> 00:25:06.560
management application that runs
on hundreds of nodes in GKE.

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:10.970
In the second example, a Fortune
50 financial services firm

00:25:10.970 --> 00:25:13.430
runs containerized
analytics services

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:17.720
on dozens of nodes in GKE
on-demand to better leverage

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:21.410
data that has already been
warehoused in GCP, thereby

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:23.086
driving greater ROI.

00:25:23.086 --> 00:25:26.900
And in the third example,
a global 200 enterprise

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:31.000
has re-architected its
SAS e-commerce platform,

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:33.050
a platform that serves
small and medium

00:25:33.050 --> 00:25:35.960
sized businesses by providing
them with everything

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:40.080
from a storefront to billing
and order processing functions.

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:44.060
They've re-architected that
platform atop Kubernetes.

00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:46.010
They run it on-premises
today, with a plan

00:25:46.010 --> 00:25:49.310
to move it into
Google Cloud, and it's

00:25:49.310 --> 00:25:51.410
designed to scale to
thousands of nodes

00:25:51.410 --> 00:25:54.350
across dozens of clusters.

00:25:54.350 --> 00:26:00.540
Now, Alex talked a lot about
infrastructure modernization.

00:26:00.540 --> 00:26:02.720
So at the same time
these businesses

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:06.437
selected GKE and Kubernetes
to drive that modernization,

00:26:06.437 --> 00:26:09.020
they also had to figure out how
to address the unique security

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:11.810
challenges that were
introduced by containers.

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:14.480
Existing security tools
lack container awareness

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:16.580
and are rendered obsolete.

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:19.490
The attack surface expands
beyond just containers

00:26:19.490 --> 00:26:22.660
themselves to encompass layers
that include the container

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:26.180
runtime, the orchestrator,
namely Kubernetes,

00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:28.280
the registry, and so on.

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:31.430
And so there's the emergence
of new threat vectors

00:26:31.430 --> 00:26:33.650
that need to be
protected against.

00:26:33.650 --> 00:26:36.470
And then, finally, the
short, ephemeral lifetimes

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:38.450
of containers make
necessary functions

00:26:38.450 --> 00:26:41.330
such as auditing
response and forensics,

00:26:41.330 --> 00:26:44.310
in particular,
much, much harder.

00:26:44.310 --> 00:26:45.860
And so, to solve
these challenges,

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:48.614
requires modernizing
security as well.

00:26:48.614 --> 00:26:50.030
And there are a
lot of great talks

00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:52.400
going on this week
about security

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:53.830
for Kubernetes and GKE.

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:56.210
I encourage you to
check out those talks.

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:58.220
What stands out
is how Kubernetes

00:26:58.220 --> 00:27:02.090
is modernizing security
with the breadth of baseline

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:06.200
capabilities that are being
built into the platform itself.

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:08.480
There's firewalling
for pods and services,

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:10.130
there's TLS throughout
the cluster,

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:12.200
there's access control,
and audit logging,

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:15.390
and secrets management,
and storage and master.

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:19.660
GKE takes all of this,
operationalizes it for you,

00:27:19.660 --> 00:27:23.010
and augments it by giving you
a minimal container optimized

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:26.550
operating system by
managing the master for you

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:29.520
and also integrating with
container analysis and image

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:32.070
scanning functionality that's
part of Google Container

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:33.540
Registry.

00:27:33.540 --> 00:27:35.550
And so, where a
customer previously

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:38.280
had to turn to several
standalone security tools

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:40.260
to implement all
these controls, they

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:43.020
can now benefit from a
single infrastructure

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:46.810
platform that makes
security fully programmable.

00:27:46.810 --> 00:27:49.450
But our customers, particularly
those in financial space,

00:27:49.450 --> 00:27:50.830
still require more.

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:52.990
The move to containers
does not obviate

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:56.770
the need for security functions
like vulnerability management,

00:27:56.770 --> 00:27:59.670
configuration monitoring,
compliance, policy enforcement,

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:02.180
threat detection,
and instant response.

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:03.760
These customers
look to standalone

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:07.210
solutions like StackRox to
solve for these use cases.

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:10.180
And so, what StackRox
does is unify

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.790
this set of capabilities
into a single platform that

00:28:12.790 --> 00:28:16.660
allows our customers to run
on GKE with high assurance

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:18.976
and confidence.

00:28:18.976 --> 00:28:20.350
I'm now going to
run through some

00:28:20.350 --> 00:28:22.270
of the common
architectural patterns

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:25.510
that we've seen among
our financial customers

00:28:25.510 --> 00:28:27.120
adopting GKE.

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:29.830
And so in the first example,
that Fortune 100 bank

00:28:29.830 --> 00:28:32.410
chose to build a
new cloud native,

00:28:32.410 --> 00:28:35.440
mobile-first application
on top of GKE.

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:37.910
And what that looks like, is
there's a highly scalable web

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:41.350
tier made up of microservices
that sits behind a GCP load

00:28:41.350 --> 00:28:43.790
balancer and a reverse proxy.

00:28:43.790 --> 00:28:46.540
There's a separate
set of microservices

00:28:46.540 --> 00:28:51.040
that handles brokerage
functions, order processing,

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:53.710
reconciliation, and other tasks.

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:55.510
And that tier
interfaces directly

00:28:55.510 --> 00:28:58.140
with data that is
persisted off-cluster

00:28:58.140 --> 00:28:59.290
and is non-containerized.

00:28:59.290 --> 00:29:01.930
Everything that runs within
the cluster is stateless.

00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:04.420
The tier also
interfaces with a set

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:09.220
of internal custodial services,
as well as third party APIs.

00:29:09.220 --> 00:29:12.280
And one particular
concern of this customer

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:14.140
was that an attacker
could potentially

00:29:14.140 --> 00:29:16.990
gain a foothold
somewhere in the web tier

00:29:16.990 --> 00:29:19.450
and then subsequently move
laterally from one service

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:22.630
to another to gain access
to the brokerage tier, which

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:25.540
would then give
that person access

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:28.210
to the data or other
non-containerized bank

00:29:28.210 --> 00:29:28.950
services.

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:31.930
And so, the ability
to detect and respond

00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:34.870
to that type of threat vector
is one of the main use cases

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:39.370
that StackRox has helped
solve for that customer.

00:29:39.370 --> 00:29:42.700
In the second example, that
Fortune 50 financial services

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:45.970
firm has already been using
BigQuery and Cloud Data

00:29:45.970 --> 00:29:49.270
Flow to set up a highly
efficient data pipeline that

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:54.100
handles transformation, batch
processing, and warehousing.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:57.100
And what they've added
on is a GTE environment

00:29:57.100 --> 00:30:00.040
that allows their developers
to spin-up containerized Spark

00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:00.730
applications.

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:02.290
And those Spark
applications can be

00:30:02.290 --> 00:30:05.530
used to process the data
that's already in BigQuery,

00:30:05.530 --> 00:30:08.470
or analyze it, or query
and manipulate it.

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:12.730
And GKE makes big BigQuery
integration extremely easy,

00:30:12.730 --> 00:30:17.280
literally via a drop-down
selection in the console.

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:19.450
And because those
Spark services run

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:21.760
on-demand for limited
periods of time,

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:25.930
understanding what is
running, what has already run,

00:30:25.930 --> 00:30:30.880
and how policies are enforced
on services that are processing

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:33.970
different data sets with
varying degrees of sensitivity

00:30:33.970 --> 00:30:35.500
becomes a very hard challenge.

00:30:35.500 --> 00:30:37.420
And so that is one
of the main areas

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:40.850
that StackRox has helped
solve for them as well.

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:43.990
And then finally, that
global 200 business

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:47.680
that has containerized its
existing e-commerce platform

00:30:47.680 --> 00:30:48.736
on-premises.

00:30:48.736 --> 00:30:50.110
This is really an
effort designed

00:30:50.110 --> 00:30:52.960
to drive application
portability and agility,

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:56.890
and is part of a plan to
eventually move to Google Cloud

00:30:56.890 --> 00:31:00.760
and GKE as part of a broader
lift and shift migration.

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:03.940
Now, what stands out here
is that these are not

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:07.540
microservices, these are legacy
services-- think TomCat, think

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:08.230
WebLogic.

00:31:08.230 --> 00:31:10.180
These are not stripped
down services built

00:31:10.180 --> 00:31:13.690
from minimal base OS images.

00:31:13.690 --> 00:31:16.455
And so vulnerability
management, and handling

00:31:16.455 --> 00:31:18.520
those configurations
and compliance

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:22.300
become key critical use cases
that the customer and us

00:31:22.300 --> 00:31:24.760
initially focused on.

00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:28.930
Separate services are run to
handle logging and laundering.

00:31:28.930 --> 00:31:29.860
These are off-cluster.

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:31.690
These are non-containerized.

00:31:31.690 --> 00:31:36.490
But as part of the
organization's move to GKE,

00:31:36.490 --> 00:31:39.070
they anticipate adopting
the stack driver [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:39.070 --> 00:31:44.770
which Alex described
for those functions.

00:31:44.770 --> 00:31:46.750
As a proud partner
of Google Cloud,

00:31:46.750 --> 00:31:49.510
we've invested in delivering
a seamless experience

00:31:49.510 --> 00:31:51.340
across StackRox and GKE.

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:53.950
We have native integration
that automatically

00:31:53.950 --> 00:31:57.696
sends alert information out to
Cloud Security Command Center

00:31:57.696 --> 00:31:59.320
so that you can view
threat information

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:03.490
about your containers alongside
all of your other security data

00:32:03.490 --> 00:32:05.980
about all your other
cloud resources.

00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:08.560
And also, to re-emphasize,
for a moment,

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:11.470
the importance and
significance of discovery

00:32:11.470 --> 00:32:14.450
and gaining visibility
when migrating to GKE,

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:17.140
it is the starting
point for nearly all

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:20.140
of the financial customers
that we engage with.

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:23.500
Being able to understand what
is running, what has run,

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:26.620
and to do so in a context
that abstracts away

00:32:26.620 --> 00:32:29.320
potentially thousands and
thousands of containers.

00:32:29.320 --> 00:32:31.660
Security operators do not
want to immediately know

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:33.964
what is going on with each
and every single container,

00:32:33.964 --> 00:32:36.130
rather they need to be made
aware of whether there's

00:32:36.130 --> 00:32:40.270
been a particular incident or
threat on a given application.

00:32:43.111 --> 00:32:44.860
And one of the unique
ways that we provide

00:32:44.860 --> 00:32:46.540
comprehensive visibility
for customers,

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:48.970
is we interface with every key
layer of the full container

00:32:48.970 --> 00:32:49.470
stack.

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:52.840
And so, we interface with the
operating system, the runtime,

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:55.070
the orchestrator, the
registry, and so on,

00:32:55.070 --> 00:32:57.160
so that we help build a
bigger picture and deeper

00:32:57.160 --> 00:32:59.770
picture of how your
GKE environment is

00:32:59.770 --> 00:33:02.775
changing over time.

00:33:02.775 --> 00:33:04.900
So what have we gleaned
from the past several years

00:33:04.900 --> 00:33:07.847
of working with the
financial services community?

00:33:07.847 --> 00:33:09.430
Here are some common
practices that we

00:33:09.430 --> 00:33:10.990
see nearly all adhere to.

00:33:10.990 --> 00:33:13.750
The first, is they start
with hardening their cluster

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:15.700
infrastructure,
utilizing controls

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:17.770
that are built into
communities and GKE,

00:33:17.770 --> 00:33:21.490
such as role-based access
control, node constraints,

00:33:21.490 --> 00:33:25.060
service account privileges,
network policies, metadata

00:33:25.060 --> 00:33:27.190
concealment, and so on.

00:33:27.190 --> 00:33:28.670
It's a very long
list, which speaks

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:30.515
to the richness of
security capabilities

00:33:30.515 --> 00:33:33.580
that are being built
into the platform itself.

00:33:33.580 --> 00:33:36.880
Next, they focus on ensuring
that their images are

00:33:36.880 --> 00:33:39.850
securely built
and secure to use,

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:41.300
that they meet
best practices, and

00:33:41.300 --> 00:33:43.450
that the runtime
environment is configured

00:33:43.450 --> 00:33:45.730
against known benchmarks.

00:33:45.730 --> 00:33:47.110
Automation is key here.

00:33:47.110 --> 00:33:49.690
Automation and programmability,
those are the same principles

00:33:49.690 --> 00:33:52.090
that are driving
instructor modernization,

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:54.910
also have to extend to security.

00:33:54.910 --> 00:33:58.660
And finally, they then focus on
analyzing container activity.

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:00.070
Once your containers
are running,

00:34:00.070 --> 00:34:01.990
you can actually
analyze their behavior

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:04.090
to better identify
threats, because

00:34:04.090 --> 00:34:07.630
the declarative and immutable
attributes of containers

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:10.750
make it easier to spot when
an attacker is potentially

00:34:10.750 --> 00:34:14.889
looking to gain a foothold,
escalate privileges, persist

00:34:14.889 --> 00:34:18.040
within the environment, move
laterally across services,

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:20.590
or achieve some type of
objectives, such as crypto

00:34:20.590 --> 00:34:24.440
mining, or exfiltrating data.

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:26.199
And regardless of
where your organization

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:29.110
is on its container journey,
here are a set of key lessons

00:34:29.110 --> 00:34:31.239
from our customer
success stories

00:34:31.239 --> 00:34:32.989
that you can act on today.

00:34:32.989 --> 00:34:34.929
The first is that the
security controls that

00:34:34.929 --> 00:34:36.969
are built into
Kubernetes and GKE

00:34:36.969 --> 00:34:41.050
provide the starting point
for any comprehensive security

00:34:41.050 --> 00:34:42.670
program.

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:45.670
Identify what else is
needed, where, to then

00:34:45.670 --> 00:34:48.940
solve for additional use
cases, such as threat detection

00:34:48.940 --> 00:34:51.420
and response.

00:34:51.420 --> 00:34:54.790
Build in automation and apply
automation, fast iteration,

00:34:54.790 --> 00:34:58.660
and closer collaboration between
security and DevOps teams

00:34:58.660 --> 00:35:02.150
as part of your organizational
workflows to better prevent,

00:35:02.150 --> 00:35:04.820
detect, and respond to threats.

00:35:04.820 --> 00:35:07.780
And then, finally, tune
your security policies

00:35:07.780 --> 00:35:10.540
based on the rich
context about containers

00:35:10.540 --> 00:35:13.270
that can be gathered
via declarative metadata

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:15.370
and runtime configuration.

00:35:15.370 --> 00:35:17.950
Collectively, these lessons
have helped our customers

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:21.340
and can also help you to better
harness all the advantages

00:35:21.340 --> 00:35:24.310
that Kubernetes and
GKE have to offer.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:30.410
We're now excited to show you
how this all comes together.

00:35:30.410 --> 00:35:33.940
I'd like to invite
Connor on stage,

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:36.580
and he's going to
demo and walk you

00:35:36.580 --> 00:35:41.040
through three examples of
how GKE and StackRox together

00:35:41.040 --> 00:35:44.290
help protect against a set
of threats in a live GKE

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:45.382
environment.

00:35:45.382 --> 00:35:47.350
CONNOR GILBERT: Thank, Wei.

00:35:47.350 --> 00:35:48.010
I'm Connor.

00:35:48.010 --> 00:35:51.040
I'm excited to show you a live
GKE cluster, as Wei mentioned,

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:54.070
and demo three real-world
scenarios of container

00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:55.050
security.

00:35:55.050 --> 00:35:56.860
I could talk to you
about this for hours,

00:35:56.860 --> 00:35:59.085
especially about how much
container technology,

00:35:59.085 --> 00:36:02.230
Kubernetes, and the integrations
with Google products

00:36:02.230 --> 00:36:04.300
can really help you get
all the right visibility

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:05.475
in the right places.

00:36:05.475 --> 00:36:08.442
But I have about five minutes,
so I'm going to dive right in.

00:36:08.442 --> 00:36:10.400
So I'm going to show you
these three scenarios.

00:36:10.400 --> 00:36:13.840
They're based on exposures
at Tesla, Shopify,

00:36:13.840 --> 00:36:15.670
and inspired by
the Equifax attack.

00:36:15.670 --> 00:36:18.580
I don't think they
were using GKE.

00:36:18.580 --> 00:36:21.940
So on the screen you've got
a StackRox portal running

00:36:21.940 --> 00:36:23.440
in the left hand
side and a terminal

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:25.550
that I'll be using on the right.

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:28.670
So let's start with
the Tesla example.

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:30.160
So this is an
example where there

00:36:30.160 --> 00:36:33.100
was an operational mistake,
or a cluster misconfiguration,

00:36:33.100 --> 00:36:36.070
and that led to a cryptojacking
attack discovered by a security

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:38.350
firm who was just scanning
the internet for Kubernetes

00:36:38.350 --> 00:36:40.150
dashboards.

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:44.230
So first we're going to
expose the dashboard here.

00:36:44.230 --> 00:36:48.000
This Kubernetes
dashboard service,

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:49.690
it can be helpful
for debugging, but it

00:36:49.690 --> 00:36:51.260
can be a security exposure.

00:36:51.260 --> 00:36:53.010
It doesn't run with
public addresses cert,

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:55.630
so I'm just going
to click through.

00:36:55.630 --> 00:36:58.660
And you can see, you can log
in with a kubeconfig or a token

00:36:58.660 --> 00:37:02.440
if you're going to use your
privileges in proxy to the API.

00:37:02.440 --> 00:37:04.170
But you can also skip.

00:37:04.170 --> 00:37:06.850
And if you do skip, it's going
to use the default service

00:37:06.850 --> 00:37:09.235
account that the dashboard
service is running under.

00:37:09.235 --> 00:37:11.527
Now, you can see there
all these errors.

00:37:11.527 --> 00:37:13.360
You were trying to debug
something, and know

00:37:13.360 --> 00:37:15.089
your screen is just
full of errors.

00:37:15.089 --> 00:37:17.380
You were supposed to be fixing
these, not getting more.

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:20.140
So you Google this,
you find out, OK,

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:24.500
this user account cannot list
persistent volume claims.

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:26.650
And you'll find some YAMLs
and some instructions

00:37:26.650 --> 00:37:29.710
about how to give that service
account cluster admin access.

00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:31.251
And this is actually,
I believe, what

00:37:31.251 --> 00:37:32.543
happened in that Tesla example.

00:37:32.543 --> 00:37:35.001
So I'll go ahead and elevate
permissions for that dashboard

00:37:35.001 --> 00:37:35.830
service account.

00:37:35.830 --> 00:37:38.470
And if I refresh the page,
I can see everything.

00:37:38.470 --> 00:37:39.670
I can debug my problem.

00:37:39.670 --> 00:37:42.810
And then I go away, and
I forget to take it off.

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:45.930
So now, anyone who goes
to this node port address

00:37:45.930 --> 00:37:49.680
will be able to come
in and use this helpful

00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:52.110
create button that is up in
the right hand corner, where

00:37:52.110 --> 00:37:53.820
I can then create deployments.

00:37:53.820 --> 00:37:56.640
So I'm just go ahead
and create a YAML--

00:37:56.640 --> 00:37:57.780
create a deployment.

00:37:57.780 --> 00:38:00.360
It is running a
crypto mining service.

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:02.936
And upload, and there we go.

00:38:02.936 --> 00:38:04.560
I'm going to head
back to the dashboard

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:07.350
and show you that we
will have detected

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:10.680
a new service coming up, and
then the crypto miner being

00:38:10.680 --> 00:38:12.140
run.

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:14.340
Wei talked about
software, supply chain,

00:38:14.340 --> 00:38:17.720
and what's in your
images and everything,

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:19.650
but sometimes you
do need to know

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:21.790
what is actually happening.

00:38:21.790 --> 00:38:23.430
Because I just
introduced an image

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:25.122
from a publicly
available registry that

00:38:25.122 --> 00:38:26.580
wasn't the one that
you were using,

00:38:26.580 --> 00:38:29.310
so you wouldn't have any sort
of scanned data or anything.

00:38:29.310 --> 00:38:30.720
You can see there's a error.

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:32.670
I'm running minor d
inside that process.

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:36.750
All the metadata that you'd need
to go respond to that issue.

00:38:36.750 --> 00:38:40.450
So I'm going to continue on.

00:38:40.450 --> 00:38:43.710
The countermeasure here
is really, don't do this.

00:38:43.710 --> 00:38:45.240
It's easy to make
these mistakes,

00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:46.950
and the defaults
are getting better,

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:48.450
but you might have
an old cluster,

00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:49.866
and it's really
important to audit

00:38:49.866 --> 00:38:51.512
who has cluster admin
and the exposure

00:38:51.512 --> 00:38:53.220
of the important
services, especially out

00:38:53.220 --> 00:38:54.549
of kubesystem namespace.

00:38:54.549 --> 00:38:56.340
You can even disable
the dashboard entirely

00:38:56.340 --> 00:38:57.660
in recent GKE releases.

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:02.254
All right, we'll move
on to Shopify now.

00:39:02.254 --> 00:39:04.170
So I just first want to
give credit to Shopify

00:39:04.170 --> 00:39:05.790
for helping educate
the community

00:39:05.790 --> 00:39:07.500
about this kind of issue.

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:09.810
They posted this as a
bug bounty and HackerOne,

00:39:09.810 --> 00:39:12.226
and made it publicly accessible
so that everyone can learn

00:39:12.226 --> 00:39:14.830
from what happened to them.

00:39:14.830 --> 00:39:18.079
So let's move on to
that demonstration.

00:39:18.079 --> 00:39:19.620
The vulnerability
there was that they

00:39:19.620 --> 00:39:21.411
had a service that was
vulnerable to server

00:39:21.411 --> 00:39:22.320
side request forgery.

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:24.660
You could ask it to
make network requests.

00:39:24.660 --> 00:39:27.510
And those network requests
could include internal resources

00:39:27.510 --> 00:39:30.960
and sensitive things, like the
cloud provider metadata server

00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:33.160
that's running on every node.

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:36.120
So I'm going to start by just
deploying an application that's

00:39:36.120 --> 00:39:39.600
sort truly vulnerable to
server side request forgery.

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:43.800
And the first thing
I'll do is have it just

00:39:43.800 --> 00:39:45.340
check what IP it's running on.

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:47.280
So you can see, first
it'll show my laptop IP,

00:39:47.280 --> 00:39:50.664
and then we should be able to
see the IP that the container

00:39:50.664 --> 00:39:52.080
cluster is actually
running under.

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:53.110
So there's my laptop.

00:39:53.110 --> 00:39:55.110
And then this is actually
the container node

00:39:55.110 --> 00:39:57.405
that the container is
going to egress from.

00:39:57.405 --> 00:40:01.080
So you can tell
they're different.

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:04.590
So then, that's not super
interesting, but what is

00:40:04.590 --> 00:40:07.440
is starting to use the
orchestrator against itself.

00:40:07.440 --> 00:40:10.920
So you can, if you make
this kind of request,

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:13.680
talk to the local
Kubelet read-only API,

00:40:13.680 --> 00:40:16.000
and enumerate things about
the cluster that you're in.

00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:19.380
So, for instance, I've just gone
and enumerated the images that

00:40:19.380 --> 00:40:21.069
are running on this machine.

00:40:21.069 --> 00:40:22.860
And this actually
highlights the importance

00:40:22.860 --> 00:40:26.010
of segmentation and separating
sensitive workloads that

00:40:26.010 --> 00:40:27.250
was mentioned earlier.

00:40:27.250 --> 00:40:29.340
You can use built in
Kubernetes features that

00:40:29.340 --> 00:40:31.940
are fully available in
GKE to make separate node

00:40:31.940 --> 00:40:35.445
pools for sensitive data,
apply taints and tolerations.

00:40:35.445 --> 00:40:37.820
So you taint a node and then
tolerate it in the workload.

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:40.111
And you can say that only
sensitive things can schedule

00:40:40.111 --> 00:40:42.090
on these sets of
nodes, so that nothing

00:40:42.090 --> 00:40:44.880
like my dumb, little app
can be scheduled next

00:40:44.880 --> 00:40:49.230
to your sensitive data and
then be used to steal it.

00:40:49.230 --> 00:40:51.600
And so that's an example
of using the Kubelet

00:40:51.600 --> 00:40:54.100
API against the cluster.

00:40:54.100 --> 00:40:55.980
The other example,
specifically with Shopify,

00:40:55.980 --> 00:40:57.540
was using the Kubem.

00:40:57.540 --> 00:41:00.370
So you can see there's
a bunch of stuff here.

00:41:00.370 --> 00:41:02.460
Not all of it
immediately decipherable.

00:41:02.460 --> 00:41:04.922
But eventually the bug
report includes the fact

00:41:04.922 --> 00:41:06.630
that there's a key
and a certificate that

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:09.510
can be used for TLS
bootstrapping with the Kubelet.

00:41:09.510 --> 00:41:12.090
This is another thing where
GKE has an counter-measure

00:41:12.090 --> 00:41:13.240
but it wasn't enabled.

00:41:13.240 --> 00:41:15.240
So there's a feature
called metadata concealment

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:17.531
in their long-term plans to
distribute cluster metadata

00:41:17.531 --> 00:41:19.020
more securely.

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:22.260
But you just need to opt in
to that kind of concealment

00:41:22.260 --> 00:41:24.684
to stop this kind of attack.

00:41:24.684 --> 00:41:26.850
You can see on the left,
we're monitoring for things

00:41:26.850 --> 00:41:28.260
like cloud metadata access.

00:41:28.260 --> 00:41:32.460
So this container ended up
contacting the cloud metadata

00:41:32.460 --> 00:41:32.980
server.

00:41:32.980 --> 00:41:36.420
And that is not in it's normal
behavior, and so we've alerted.

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:41.580
I'm going to move on now
to the Equifax attack--

00:41:41.580 --> 00:41:44.762
this is inspired by it, but
not an exact reproduction.

00:41:44.762 --> 00:41:46.470
I don't think they
were using containers,

00:41:46.470 --> 00:41:48.180
and I don't think
they were using GKE.

00:41:48.180 --> 00:41:50.220
But it's an example of where
application vulnerabilities

00:41:50.220 --> 00:41:52.470
really can hit you even
though you're in containers.

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:54.870
Containers have all these
rich configuration options--

00:41:54.870 --> 00:41:57.840
running as different users,
running with read-only rootfs,

00:41:57.840 --> 00:41:59.940
abstracting away the
access to secret things,

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:01.710
abstracting storage
away from you.

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:03.510
But you can still
write an app that

00:42:03.510 --> 00:42:07.200
gives people access and have
not configured the security

00:42:07.200 --> 00:42:10.620
boundaries well enough
that they can keep going.

00:42:10.620 --> 00:42:12.660
So I'm going to
deploy a app that

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:17.370
is vulnerable to a Struts CVE
that allows remote command

00:42:17.370 --> 00:42:18.821
execution.

00:42:18.821 --> 00:42:19.570
So that's created.

00:42:19.570 --> 00:42:22.500
And I've created a
sensitive database service

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:24.015
that I'll then contact.

00:42:24.015 --> 00:42:26.340
So I'm going to go ahead
and just make a crafted HTTP

00:42:26.340 --> 00:42:30.780
request that injects a command,
and connects to that database,

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:32.280
and dumps some
information about it.

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:35.140
The database is
the tables inside.

00:42:35.140 --> 00:42:39.610
So the port for it is still--

00:42:39.610 --> 00:42:41.940
I have to do this live.

00:42:41.940 --> 00:42:43.900
So we get to pull the pods.

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:45.150
Now, I'm going to start again.

00:42:48.270 --> 00:42:49.710
So you can see the
data leak here.

00:42:49.710 --> 00:42:53.220
So we're able to make this
large, exciting, crafted HTTP

00:42:53.220 --> 00:42:56.490
request that included
some MySQL commands.

00:42:56.490 --> 00:42:59.040
And then the output
was all the databases

00:42:59.040 --> 00:43:00.790
in the database, and
then all the tables.

00:43:00.790 --> 00:43:02.490
And if they were
more stuff in there,

00:43:02.490 --> 00:43:04.890
you could have executed
commands like that also.

00:43:04.890 --> 00:43:06.580
Then you can see on
the left-hand side,

00:43:06.580 --> 00:43:08.080
we've alerted on a
lateral movement,

00:43:08.080 --> 00:43:10.710
because these services don't
typically talk in this manner.

00:43:10.710 --> 00:43:12.630
So just an example
of runtime monitoring

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:17.460
of those connectivity graphs and
to catch the lateral movement.

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:19.729
Once they've gotten
the initial foothold,

00:43:19.729 --> 00:43:21.270
they're trying to
get the objectives.

00:43:21.270 --> 00:43:23.061
They're trying to get
through to your data.

00:43:23.061 --> 00:43:25.890
They're trying to get the
things that they want.

00:43:25.890 --> 00:43:28.690
So I'm going to hand it
back to Allan after this.

00:43:28.690 --> 00:43:31.830
I'm excited to have shown you
three real-world scenarios

00:43:31.830 --> 00:43:34.050
in a GKE environment,
and the way

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:37.380
that both GKE defaults and
StackRox-type protection

00:43:37.380 --> 00:43:38.920
can help secure your workloads.

00:43:38.920 --> 00:43:41.204
Thanks.

